WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.640
"The Effects of Cleaning Products and
Air Fresheners on Lung Function"

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.160
In a recent review of the “Damaging
effects of household cleaning

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.360
products on the lungs”, researchers
noted that the “[a]dverse

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.160
respiratory effects of cleaning
products were first observed

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.920
in populations experiencing high level[s]
of exposure at the workplace,

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.640
such as cleaners and
health-care workers,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.560
with a primary focus on asthma.”
But the occupational use of

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.760
disinfectants has also been linked
to a higher risk of developing

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.400
chronic obstructive pulmonary
diseases like emphysema.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.000
And now we know it’s not just
workplace exposures but also

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.360
“common household exposures”
that are “risk factor[s] for

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.480
respiratory disorders in
childhood”, as well potentially

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.920
an important risk factor for
adult asthma, with common

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.480
household cleaning spray use
accounting for as many as

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.440
one in seven adult asthma cases.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.120
The thought is that the inhalation
of chemical irritants may cause

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.440
injury of the airways that leads to
oxidative stress and inflammation.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.320
Okay, so what can we do about it?

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.360
Well, it may indeed be limited to sprays.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.120
Cleaning products not applied in
spray form were not associated

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.400
with asthma, and it’s possible
that environmentally friendly

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.200
cleaning products may
represent a safer alternative,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.346
though they may still present some risk.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.680
Ideally, safer cleaning products
should be available.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.680
Unfortunately, the research suggesting
harm “has seldom been heeded

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.967
by manufactures, vendors, and
commercial cleaning companies.”

00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:38.480
I wonder how much of that is
because “most of the workers

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.040
exposed to cleaning product
[both occupationally

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.800
and presumably domestically,]
are women.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.760
One of the problems may be
the fragrance chemicals.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.960
One in three Americans surveyed
“reported health problems

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.160
such as migraine headaches
and respiratory difficulties,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.960
when exposed to fragranced products.”

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.000
And in about half it was so bad
they actually lost work over it.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.440
“Results from this study reveal
that more than one third

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.000
of Americans suffer adverse
health effects, such as

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.240
respiratory difficulties
and migraine headaches,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.640
from exposure to fragranced products.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.040
Of [all] individuals, half reported
that the effects can be disabling.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.840
Yet [more than] 99 percent
of Americans are exposed

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.000
to fragranced products at
least once a week . . .”

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.480
The effect on asthmatics
may be even worse,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.560
affecting closer to two-thirds.
One compound that may be

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.120
of particular concern is
called 1, 4-dichlorobenzene,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.880
also known as para-dichlorobenzene,
which is found in many

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.746
air fresheners, toilet bowl
deodorants, and mothballs.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.920
It breaks down in the body into a
compound called 2,5-dichlorophenol,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.760
which you pee out, giving
researchers a reliable measure

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.480
of your dichlorobenzene exposure.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.320
Not only may it make respiratory
problems worse for those already

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.480
suffering from compromised airways,
but exposure to dichlorobenzene

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.360
“at blood levels found in
the general U.S. population,

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.840
may result in reduced [lung] function”
in people who start out

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.040
with normal breathing. What’s worse,
higher exposure was associated

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.840
with greater prevalence of
cardiovascular disease and cancer.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.560
That’s not good!
So, better read the labels, right?

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.080
Surprisingly, there is “no law
in the U.S. [that] requires

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.680
the disclosure of all ingredients
in fragranced consumer products”.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.920
In fact, for air fresheners, laundry
supplies, and cleaning products

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.520
they don’t even need to say
it has fragrance at all.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.618
You don’t know until you smell it.

00:03:39.618 --> 00:03:43.759
So, if you can’t tell which
products have which chemicals,

00:03:43.759 --> 00:03:46.772
you can follow the lead of the CDC,

00:03:46.772 --> 00:03:48.960
the Centers for Disease
Control and Prevention.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.720
Not only is cigarette smoking
indoors prohibited at all times,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.960
“[s]cented or fragranced products
are [also] prohibited at all times

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.760
in all interior space owned,
rented, or leased by CDC.”

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.040
I wish rideshare services
like Uber and Lyft would have

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.480
a similar policy or at least
a fragrance-free option.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.600
About one in five or more than
a thousand Americans surveyed

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.160
said they would turn right
around and leave a business

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.760
if they smelled air fresheners
or some fragranced product,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.959
so it’s in business’s best
interest too, since “[more than]

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:24.160
50 percent of the population
would prefer that workplaces,

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.960
health care facilities, and [their
health care] professionals,

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.327
hotels, and airplanes
were fragrance-free.”

